_model: proposition
---
title: Example page
---
order: 7
---
short_label: Example
---
sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: lemma
----
label: A lemma box
----
proof:

This page is a working example of the structure the rest of the site is written
in. It uses the real proposition model and the real template; nothing here is
mocked up in HTML.

A page's body is a list of sections, and each section renders as its own box.
Sections of kind `lemma` come first, before the proposition, because that is the
order Euclid's text puts them in.

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: A statement field holds the claim, set apart at the head of its box and quoted by other pages.
----
proof:

The proof field is ordinary markdown with three additions.

The first is an element reference. Writing {A} or {AB} in the prose binds it to
the figure alongside — hover the letters here and the matching part of the
diagram lights up, and hovering the diagram works in reverse.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="330" height="250" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // The construction of I.1, used here only because it is the smallest
    // figure that has something worth stepping through.
    var START = ["A","B","AB"];
    var CIRC = START.concat(["BCD","ACE"]);
    var APEX = CIRC.concat(["C","AC","BC"]);
    var DONE = APEX.concat(["ABC"]);
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "Example figure",
        pivot: "C",
        elements: [
            "A;point;free;110,160",
            "B;point;free;210,160",
            "AB;line;connect;A,B",
            "BCD;circle;radius;A,B",
            "ACE;circle;radius;B,A",
            "CD;line;bichord;BCD,ACE;0;0;0",
            "C;point;first;CD",
            "AC;line;connect;A,C",
            "BC;line;connect;B,C",
            "ABC;polygon;triangle;A,B,C;0;0;black;cyan"
        ],
        initiallyHidden: ["BCD","ACE","CD","C","AC","BC","ABC"],
        slides: [
            { text: "A slide names which elements are visible and which are highlighted. Here, only the given line {AB}.",
              visible: START,
              highlighted: ["AB"] },
            { text: "Describe the two circles. A transition animates the step rather than cutting to it.",
              visible: CIRC,
              highlighted: ["BCD","ACE"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "BCD", name: geomlib.A.Circle.compass },
                  { elem: "ACE", name: geomlib.A.Circle.compass } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.Post.3" }] },
            { text: "Join {AC} and {BC}, where the circles cut at {C}.",
              visible: APEX,
              highlighted: ["AC","BC","C"],
              transition: { mode: "parallel", animations: [
                  { elem: "AC", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "BC", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.Post.1" }] },
            { text: "The triangle {ABC} is equilateral. A justification on a slide links out the same way a marginal one does.",
              visible: DONE,
              highlighted: ["ABC"],
              transition: { animations: [ { elem: "ABC", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outlineAndFill } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.Def.15" }, { ref: "Q.E.D." }] }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });

    window.eucrefs = (window.eucrefs || {});
    Object.assign(window.eucrefs, {
        "I.Post.1":  "/elements/books/bookI/postulates/post1/",
        "I.Post.3":  "/elements/books/bookI/postulates/post3/",
        "I.Def.15":  "/elements/books/bookI/definitions/defI15_18/"
    });
</script>
</figure>

The second addition is a justification. A step that leans on something earlier
names it, and it renders in the margin beside the step rather than interrupting
it. [!just I.Post.3]

The same figure also carries a slide walk. The element list above is the only
description of the geometry; the slides say which parts of it are on screen at
each step, so the static diagram and the stepped proof cannot disagree. Press
the present control under the figure to walk it. [!just I.Post.1]

The third addition is the citation token used in commentary rather than proof —
`@I.47` resolves to whichever URL that proposition currently has, so the prose
survives a change of scheme.

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
----
guide:

The guide field is commentary on the page as a whole. It renders once, below
every box, under its own heading — so a page reads as its boxes in order and
then its commentary, wherever the proposition section happens to sit among them.

This is where a citation like @I.1 belongs: prose that discusses the text rather
than proves something in it.

#### prop_section ####
kind: note
----
label: A note box
----
guide:

A section of kind `note` or `remark` carries its commentary inside its own box,
because the box *is* the remark. These come after the proposition, matching the
layout of the books that have them.

Below this you would normally see a *referenced by* table, listing the pages
whose justifications point here. It is empty on this page: the reverse index is
built by walking the text itself, so an example living outside it neither
appears in that index nor inserts itself into anyone else's.
